.TH TLSCHECK 1 "2026-05-23" "hako" "hako utilities"
.SH NAME
tlscheck \- verify a TLS handshake
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tlscheck
.RB [ \-\-cert | \-\-chain | \-\-expiry | \-\-fingerprint ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-p\ port ]
.I host[:port]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tlscheck
connects to a TLS service, verifies the server certificate against the system
trust store, verifies the certificate name against
.I host
or the name supplied with
.BR \-\-name ,
and prints a short success line or requested certificate details.
.PP
The default port is
.BR 443 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-cert
Print the leaf certificate subject, issuer, validity window, DNS names, and
verification status.
.TP
.B \-\-chain
Print a one-line subject and issuer summary for each certificate sent by the
server.
.TP
.B \-\-expiry
Print the leaf certificate expiration time.
.TP
.B \-\-fingerprint
Print the SHA-256 fingerprint of the leaf certificate.
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " name
Use
.I name
for SNI and certificate name verification while connecting to
.IR host .
.TP
.BI "\-p " port
Connect to
.I port
instead of the default port or the port embedded in
.IR host[:port] .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B "tlscheck example.com"
Verify the TLS service at
.BR example.com:443 .
.TP
.B "tlscheck -p 8443 example.com"
Verify the TLS service at
.BR example.com:8443 .
.TP
.B "tlscheck --cert example.com"
Print the leaf certificate summary for
.BR example.com .
.TP
.B "tlscheck --name example.com 127.0.0.1:8443"
Connect to
.B 127.0.0.1:8443
but verify the certificate for
.BR example.com .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The TLS handshake and certificate verification succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong arguments, connection failure, or certificate verification failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fetch (1),
.BR hako (1)
